## Examples of Blunders\n\
\n\
Here are some examples of the kinds of mistakes to flag:\n\
\n\
- Not attacking for lethal, missing combo kills, burn in hand at low life\n\
- Casting spells that accomplish nothing, cards with no valid targets, declining pure-upside abilities\n\
- Removing the wrong threat, fetching the wrong land, naming the wrong card\n\
- Casting spells before playing lands, creatures before combat when holding tricks\n\
- Poor attack/block decisions, attacking into unfavorable blocks\n\
- Missing land drops, not using mana sinks at end of opponent's turn\n\
- Fundamentally wrong game plan decisions, not countering must-answer threats\n\
- Overextending into board wipes, running best threat into open counter mana\n\
- Passing priority in the postcombat main phase (with nothing on the stack) when there are still sorcery-speed actions available this turn — e.g. unplayed land drops, castable creatures or sorceries in hand, planeswalker abilities to activate. Passing here ends the turn and wastes those opportunities. Note: the choices list shows the exact legal actions available — if a player passes (Chosen: False) with playable lands or castable spells among the choices, that is strong evidence of a blunder.\n\
\n\
## Understanding \"Chosen: False\"\n\
\n\
\"Chosen: False\" means the player passed priority — they declined to act. If the stack is empty, passing means moving to the next phase (e.g. main phase to combat, or postcombat main to end step — ending the turn). If the stack has items, passing lets those items resolve without responding.\n\
\n\
## Understanding \"Chosen: (no response)\"\n\
\n\
\"Chosen: (no response)\" means the player failed to respond in time (timeout) or their client did not send a valid action. The game engine chose a default for them — typically passing or skipping. Treat this like \"Chosen: False\" for blunder evaluation: if skipping was wrong given the available choices, flag it.\n\
\n\
## Understanding batch/text decisions\n\
\n\
Some decisions (attack/block declarations, color choices) use batch or text parameters instead of selecting from a numbered list. These show as \"Chosen: Attack with: ...\", \"Chosen: Block with: ...\", or \"Chosen: Text: ...\" instead of a choice name. These are valid responses — the player DID act.\n\
\n\
## Severity Levels\n\
\n\
- **questionable**: Probably suboptimal but debatable. A human reviewing the game would find this interesting to think about. Use this when there's at least a ~30% chance the play was wrong. Low bar — when in doubt, include as questionable rather than omitting.\n\
- **minor**: Clearly suboptimal — a small amount of value was lost (e.g. slightly wrong sequencing, fetching a less optimal land, missing a minor advantage).\n\
- **moderate**: A real mistake with meaningful consequences — wasted a card, missed a significant line, or gave the opponent an unnecessary opening.\n\
- **major**: Game-losing or close to it — threw away a winning position, wasted multiple cards for nothing, missed lethal, or made an error that directly led to losing.\n\
\n\
## Output Format\n\
\n\
Return ONLY valid JSON — either `null` (no blunder) or a single annotation object:\n\
{\n\
  \"severity\": \"questionable\" | \"minor\" | \"moderate\" | \"major\",\n\
  \"description\": \"<what went wrong in concrete game terms>\",\n\
  \"action_taken\": \"<what they actually did>\",\n\
  \"better_line\": \"<what they should have done>\"\n\
}",
